  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in Maine totaled $3.1B in FY2026 to date. The federal government made 8,152 awards in the state, with an average award size of $375K. Obligations fell 22.2% year over year. The Department of Defense accounted for the largest share of buying, obligating $2.8B. Ship Building and Repairing led industry spending with $2.1B, and BATH IRON WORKS CORPORATION received $2.1B in obligations. Annual obligations rose from $2.4B in FY21 to a recent high of $3.9B in FY25, then moved to $3.1B in FY26 (partial). These figures are contract obligations and the FY2026 total is a fiscal year to date number.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in Maine changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 22.2% year over year. Obligations moved from $3.9B in FY25 to $3.1B in FY26 (partial). Obligations trended upward from $2.4B in FY21 to $3.9B in FY25 before the FY26 partial total."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate federal spending in Maine?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $2.8B in FY2026 to date. BATH IRON WORKS CORPORATION is the largest vendor with $2.1B in obligations and Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry with $2.1B. The Department of Homeland Security, Department of Agriculture, and Department of Labor follow with $79.1M, $30.7M, and $28.5M respectively."
      }
    ]
  },
